255859375, -0.03094482421875, -0.031951904296875, -0.0023365020751953125, -0.01438140869140625, 0.01092529296875, 0.0156097412109375, -0.0126953125, -0.0178375244140625, 0.0206451416015625, -0.006282806396484375, -0.0146331787109375, 0.04534912109375, 0.002044677734375, 0.0217437744140625, 0.0053863525390625, 0.0242462158203125, -0.024871826171875, 0.0269317626953125, 0.0219573974609375, -0.0007376670837402344, 0.0177001953125, -0.038299560546875, -0.01003265380859375, 0.018829345703125, -0.01458740234375, -0.0117950439453125, 0.0015478134155273438, 0.01103973388671875, 0.01354217529296875, -0.026885986328125, 0.0035343170166015625, -0.02825927734375, -0.0031299591064453125, 0.0211639404296875, -0.01708984375, 0.0156707763671875, 0.0031299591064453125, -0.022979736328125, 0.02642822265625, -0.0215606689453125, -0.0231475830078125, 0.020843505859375, 0.006256103515625, 0.0240936279296875, 0.00858306884765625, 0.016815185546875, 0.039764404296875, -0.01092529296875, -0.0011186599731445312, -0.0022602081298828125], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "\"\"\"\nPurpose  \nTo develop and evaluate a locally deployed multi-agent AI system for radiology report structuring and quality assurance (QA).\n\nMaterials and Methods  \n\nOverview of Workflow  \nThis retrospective study assessed a two-stage AI pipeline for structuring and QA of radiology reports. The system first segmented reports into anatomical sections using a rule-based agent, then refined assignments and performed QA tasks using a local large language model (LLM) agent. Performance was evaluated by radiologist review of a stratified sample of reports.\n\nStudy Design, Data, and Inclusion/Exclusion Criteria  \nA total of 638 radiology reports from CT chest, abdomen, and pelvis exams, dictated by 15 board-certified radiologists at a single institution in 2023\u20132024, were included. Inclusion criteria were: finalized (not preliminary) reports for adult patients (age \u226518 years) from routine CT chest, abdomen, and/or pelvis exams. Exclusion criteria were: addenda, preliminary or draft reports, pediatric cases, and studies with incomplete metadata or missing sections. Reports meeting these criteria were identified from the institutional PACS.\n\nSampling and Stratification  \nFor evaluation, reports were randomly sampled using a stratified approach to ensure representativeness by exam type (chest, abdomen, pelvis, or combination), reporting radiologist, and report length (short, medium, long tertiles based on sentence count). Stratification was performed by first grouping reports by exam type, then by radiologist within each group, and finally by report length tertile within each radiologist group. If a report qualified for multiple strata, assignment was prioritized in the order: exam type > radiologist > length. In cases of overlap or insufficient numbers in a stratum, random selection was used to fill remaining slots, and any conflicts were resolved by random assignment to maintain proportional representation. The final evaluation set of 45 reports was stratified to include 23 flagged and 22 unflagged reports, reflecting the overall flagged/unflagged ratio in the dataset. Sampling was performed using a random number generator, with stratification variables applied as described to maintain balance across radiologists and exam types. Potential biases include overrepresentation of certain exam types or radiologists if strata were small.\n\nMulti-Agent AI Pipeline Architecture  \n\nRule-Based Agent  \nThe first agent used regular expressions (regex) to identify and segment sentences into standardized anatomical sections (e.g., lungs, liver, kidneys). Regex patterns such as `r\"\\b(lungs?|pulmonary)\\b\"` and `r\"\\b(liver|hepatic)\\b\"` were iteratively developed by reviewing 100 randomly selected local reports. For example, initial patterns failed to capture \u201chepatic dome\u201d or \u201cpulmonary vasculature,\u201d which were added after error analysis. The final set included 18 anatomical section patterns, summarized as: lungs/pulmonary, pleura, heart/cardiac, mediastinum, liver/hepatic, gallbladder, pancreas, spleen, kidneys/renal, adrenal, bowel, bladder, uterus, prostate, bones, vessels, lymph nodes, and other. Patterns were refined to maximize recall (target >95% of anatomical section headers correctly identified) and minimize false positives (target <5% incorrect assignments), as measured by manual review on a validation set of 50 reports. These thresholds were chosen to ensure that the majority of clinically relevant sections were captured with minimal misclassification, based on prior literature and internal pilot data indicating that recall below 95% led to frequent omission of important findings. Performance was tracked by calculating the proportion of correctly and incorrectly assigned sentences per section.\n\nLLM Agent and Prompt Engineering  \nA 7B-parameter Llama-2 model, running on-premises, was used to resolve ambiguous assignments and perform semantic QA checks. The LLM was prompted with institution-specific instructions. For example, initial prompts were: \u201cAssign the following sentence to the most appropriate anatomical section: [sentence].\u201d Early versions struggled with ambiguous phrases (e.g., \u201cupper abdomen mass\u201d), leading to prompt revisions such as: \u201cUse the following section list: [list]. If uncertain, assign to \u2018Other\u2019.\u201d Radiologist feedback highlighted confusion with overlapping anatomy (e.g., \u201cadrenal\u201d vs. \u201ckidney\u201d), prompting further clarification in the prompt. The prompt engineering process involved 5 major iterations, with each version tested on a validation set of 30 reports. Prompts were revised until >90% agreement with expert assignments was achieved on the validation set. The >90% agreement threshold was selected to ensure high reliability and minimize clinically significant misassignments, consistent with standards in prior NLP studies.\n\nAgent Interaction and QA Tasks  \nA controller script orchestrated the pipeline: the regex agent generated preliminary section assignments, which the LLM agent reviewed and refined. The LLM also performed three QA tasks using prompt-based logic:  \n(a) Section mismatch: \u201cAre there findings in the Findings section not reflected in the Impression, or vice versa?\u201d  \n(b) Gender-anatomy conflict: \u201cDoes the report mention anatomical findings inconsistent with the patient\u2019s gender?\u201d  \n(c) Undocumented critical findings: \u201cIs there mention of a critical finding, and if so, is communication documented?\u201d  \nPrompts for each task were validated by comparing LLM outputs to expert annotations on 50 reports, with error rates (false positives/negatives) tracked and prompts revised to minimize errors (<10% for each task). These thresholds were set to balance sensitivity and specificity, aiming to minimize missed critical findings while avoiding excessive false alarms, and are in line with published QA tool standards.\n\nPrimary and Secondary Outcomes  \nThe primary outcome was the correct restructuring rate, defined as the proportion of reports in which all sentences were assigned to the correct anatomical section with no omission or fabrication of clinically important information. Secondary outcomes included: (1) performance of each QA task (sensitivity, specificity, false positive/negative rates), (2) inter-rater reliability (Cohen\u2019s kappa), (3) frequency and nature of reviewer disagreements, and (4) system inference time and hardware requirements.\n\nEvaluation Criteria, Inter-Rater Reliability, and Consensus  \nCorrectness of restructuring was defined as all sentences assigned to correct anatomical sections, with no omission or fabrication of clinically important information. Clinically important information was operationalized as findings that, if omitted or misassigned, could alter patient management (e.g., new or significant lesions, critical findings, actionable findings as defined by ACR guidelines). For example, a pulmonary nodule assigned to the \u201cOther\u201d section instead of \u201cLungs\u201d was considered incorrect if it could affect follow-up recommendations. Two board-certified radiologists independently assessed:  \n(1) Correctness of restructuring, using the rubric above.  \n(2) QA task performance\u2014each flagged issue was rated as true/false positive/negative.  \n(3) Omission or fabrication of clinically important information\u2014examples included missing documentation of a pulmonary nodule or erroneous addition of a finding.  \nDisagreements were resolved by consensus discussion, with each case discussed until agreement was reached. The frequency and nature of disagreements were recorded and categorized as section assignment, omission, fabrication, or ambiguous language. Of the 12 cases with initial disagreement (27%), 8 involved borderline section assignments (e.g., \u201cright upper quadrant mass\u201d could be liver or other), 3 involved omission of minor findings (e.g., \u201cmild atelectasis\u201d), and 1 involved ambiguous language. Consensus was achieved through joint review and discussion of clinical context, with both radiologists required to agree on the final categorization. The impact of these disagreements is that borderline or ambiguous cases may be subject to interpretation, potentially affecting the measured accuracy.\n\nStatistical Analysis  \nAll statistical analyses were performed using Python 3.10 with the SciPy and statsmodels libraries. Proportions and confidence intervals (CIs) were calculated using the Wilson score method. Cohen\u2019s kappa was used to assess inter-rater reliability for restructuring correctness, with kappa values interpreted according to standard thresholds (0.61\u20130.80: substantial agreement). For QA task performance, sensitivity, specificity, and 95% CIs were calculated for each task. Missing data were not present in the evaluation set; all sampled reports were complete and included in the analysis. Power analysis for the primary outcome was performed using the statsmodels \u201cpower\u201d module, assuming a binomial distribution.\n\nSystem Requirements and Deployment  \nThe system was deployed on a workstation with dual NVIDIA RTX 4090 GPUs, 256 GB RAM, and Ubuntu 22.04. LLM inference averaged 2.5 seconds per report; regex processing was <0.1 seconds per report. To benchmark feasibility, we tested the system on a standard clinical workstation (Intel Xeon CPU, 64 GB RAM, NVIDIA RTX 3060 GPU), where LLM inference time increased to 12.8 seconds per report. Model quantization and use of smaller models (e.g., 3B-parameter LLMs) reduced inference time by 40\u201360% with minimal loss in accuracy. The system operated as a standalone batch processor and was not integrated into clinical workflow. Integration strategies, including potential PACS/RIS integration, user interface development, and real-time feedback mechanisms, are discussed in the Limitations section below.\n\nResults  \n\nPrimary Outcome: Report Structuring  \nThe system structured the Findings sections of all 638 reports (22,270 sentences) into a predefined anatomical format, retaining original content. Of the 45 evaluated reports, both reviewers agreed that 31 (69%, 95% CI: 54\u201381%) were correctly restructured, 2 (4%, 95% CI: 0\u201313%) were incorrectly restructured, and 12 (27%, 95% CI: 15\u201341%) had reviewer disagreement. Correct restructuring required all sentences to be assigned to the correct anatomical section, with no omission or fabrication of clinically important information. For example, a report was considered correctly restructured if all findings (e.g., \u201c3 mm pulmonary nodule,\u201d \u201cmild hepatic steatosis\u201d) were"}}